   "snippet": "Since the US military lost access to Air Base 201 in Agadez—formerly Washington's premier ISR (Intelligence, Surveillance, and Reconnaissance) hub in the region—AFRICOM has indeed suffered a severe degradation in real-time visibility over JNIM and Islamic State in the Greater Sahara (ISGS) movements.\n\nAcross multiple administrations and high-level Pentagon visits (going back to former Defense Secretary Mark Esper's 2020 visit to Algiers and subsequent AFRICOM Memorandums of Understanding), Washington has quietly urged Algeria to abandon its strict constitutional ban on out-of-area operations (amended in 2020) and project stabilizing power southward.\n\nAlgeria's Core Motivation is that Algiers is acting purely out of national interest. The immediate catalyst for the deployment is securing Algerian state energy giant Sonatrach’s oil exploration blocks in northern Niger (specifically the Kafra block) and safeguarding the proposed Trans-Saharan Gas Pipeline corridor.",
   "body": "Since the US military lost access to Air Base 201 in Agadez—formerly Washington's premier ISR (Intelligence, Surveillance, and Reconnaissance) hub in the region—AFRICOM has indeed suffered a severe degradation in real-time visibility over JNIM and Islamic State in the Greater Sahara (ISGS) movements.\n\nAcross multiple administrations and high-level Pentagon visits (going back to former Defense Secretary Mark Esper's 2020 visit to Algiers and subsequent AFRICOM Memorandums of Understanding), Washington has quietly urged Algeria to abandon its strict constitutional ban on out-of-area operations (amended in 2020) and project stabilizing power southward.\n\nAlgeria's Core Motivation is that Algiers is acting purely out of national interest. The immediate catalyst for the deployment is securing Algerian state energy giant Sonatrach’s oil exploration blocks in northern Niger (specifically the Kafra block) and safeguarding the proposed Trans-Saharan Gas Pipeline corridor.",
